From: Dong Chenchen <dongchenchen2@huawei.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 14:20:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH openEuler-26.09 19/25] mm: control i_mmap optimizations from
 the command line

hulk inclusion
category: feature
bugzilla: https://atomgit.com/openeuler/kernel/issues/9771

--------------------------------

The preceding patches optimize file-backed VMA insertion and removal by
batching i_mmap updates, then split contended reverse-mapping trees into
NUMA-local shards. Enabling those changes unconditionally makes it harder
to compare with the original path and prevents deployments from selecting
the conservative behavior without building another kernel.

Keep CONFIG_I_MMAP_SHARDS as a build-time capability, but disable it by
default and enable it in the arm64 openEuler defconfig. Leave the x86
openEuler defconfig disabled; local x86 testing can enable the capability
in .config. Add the boot-only i_mmap_opt=<bool> parameter and enable the
optimized paths only with i_mmap_opt=on. A boot-only policy also avoids
changing the tree layout or batching semantics while mappings are live.

When the option is off, retain the original paths:

  - dup_mmap() inserts each file VMA directly under the central i_mmap
    lock;
  - exit_mmap() retains its original batching by struct file rather than
    grouping VMAs by address_space;
  - shard pointer access, VMA accounting, contention tracking, allocation,
    publication, and NUMA home-node state are skipped.

Use a default-false static key for the boot-time dispatch. Keep dup_mmap()
as a single function and select its batch storage through a local pointer;
the disabled branch performs the original direct insertion. The shard
writer wrappers similarly fall through to the central lock when disabled.

With i_mmap_opt=on, retain the batched fork and exit paths and allow
dynamic NUMA-local shard installation after lock contention.

Signed-off-by: Dong Chenchen <dongchenchen2@huawei.com>
